{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: exact head f0854d8fd123727ab509827845cbdd7871d30090 on planner/client-primitive-note-20261113, its one artifact `notes/2026-11-13-scope-client.md`, for request e5b86654 under promise 52171349 and review request f00ba3c0. The design holds, and the head changes draft 3 only by its status line, a merge of main and six line references. One rule is missing, and C1 needs it before it builds.\n\nRequired change: state what custody does when a new signing would make a ninth open envelope.\n\n- Section 5.1 caps a record at 8 envelopes and 64 signings. It refuses `full`, reason `signings`, only at the 65th signing. It keeps each earlier envelope until its signing is closed, and a signing closes only on a refusal of each copy or on a read after its death.\n- Rule 6 signs again at each submit after a refusal of class `head`. Nine submits inside one lifetime of 300 s, each refused `revision-moved`, leave nine envelopes that are not closed. The note does not say whether the ninth submit refuses, or which envelope custody drops.\n- Dropping an open envelope is not safe. Section 5.3 settles the kept envelopes after `mismatch`, and `not-found` for each makes the phase `mismatch`. If the dropped envelope is the signing that the scope sealed, the record reads `mismatch` while one of its own signings holds the key. The status of section 6 stays right, because it reads the replica, but custody does not.\n- The smallest rule: before it signs, custody refuses `full`, reason `signings`, when the record already holds 8 envelopes. The member's values stay in the draft, and the next submit after a signing closes succeeds. That matches the `full`, reason `bytes`, check that ruling 0767a3f9 R1 places before the signing. If you choose another rule, state why a dropped envelope cannot be the sealed one. Carry the rule into request C1 (21f6d3cd).\n\nThe kernel question. I confirm the note's reading of section 10. The note changes no answer shape. `mismatch` keeps its reason alone (`packages/contract/src/result.ts:81`), and `settle` keeps `not-found` (`packages/scope/src/core.ts:900-916`). `predicted` calls `judgeAct` and adds no judgment. The class of each reason is RC-1's export, under RC-1's own review. The note names no K item, and none needs `docs/kernel.md` section 7. One sentence reads otherwise: in section 10, \"It also needs the review of `docs/kernel.md` section 7\" follows \"A new member on `mismatch` breaks each older client\". The \"It\" means the new member that the note does not take. Name it, so that a reader does not take the sentence as a statement about the note.\n\nWhat I read:\n\n- The whole note at the head, and its diff from 2ef973ed: the status line and six line references. Each reference holds at main 57e893c0: `judge.ts:225-229` is the key rule, `custody.ts:406` and `:423` are the refusal and the terminal return, `result.ts:81` is the `mismatch` shape, and `core.ts:900-916` is `settle`.\n- Keys and signings (section 4) against the scope's rule. The first sealed intent of an actor holds the key for the life of the scope, the same bytes get its receipt again, and other bytes get `mismatch`. Rules 1 to 6 therefore give one entry for one request, and the client never needs to prove that an earlier signing is dead before it signs again.\n- Custody (section 5) against ruling 0767a3f9 and the builder's plan 097030b3.\n- Status (section 6), the schedule (section 7), slots (section 8) and the view (section 9).\n\nWhat I checked:\n\n- Scope. Main 57e893c0 is an ancestor. The head changes one path, the note, and the artifact covers it at the head. The artifact is neither stale nor retired.\n- Cheap checks. `check-notes` reports 0 warnings. The commit-message check passes: 6 checked, 0 flagged. `git diff --check` is clean. I ran no tests, since the head changes no code.\n\nArchitecture: sound apart from the required change. One client owns the key, the schedule, the store calls and the status, so an adapter keeps none of them. Custody stays a store with rules and no timer. The replica decides status, and custody records answers, so a stale custody phase cannot show a wrong status. The entry `@generalbusiness/dap-client/scope` keeps `dap-derive` out of the root entry.\n\nSecurity: no finding. The client signs only on a member's submit (rule 3), so no signature exists without a click. A key that is re-signed with new values cannot make two entries, because the scope admits one intent per actor and key. `folded` and `absent` take the caller's word, and the caller is the client's own verified replica. `absent` closes a signing that a slow scope clock can still seal, and section 5.3 states that case. Two notes for C1:\n\n1. `refused` records leave the 256-record cap. Each one holds at most 8 envelopes, but the note gives no bound on how many `refused` records one store keeps. State when one leaves the store.\n2. `withdrawn` and `predicted` run `judgeAct` at the replica's head in the browser. They decide only words on the page and grant nothing.\n\nSimplification: no required change. Rules 3 and 4 of section 6 both give `pending` and could be one rule: \"an answer that the replica has not reached\".\n\nThis verdict authorizes no landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0f7e868791500a93a01c2319a0587902f4836467","binding":"assigned","head":"f0854d8fd123727ab509827845cbdd7871d30090","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:8fb82b9e3fb6ee64820c36c6e219d84bb9edb6eb\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:52171349a1a04f8ff2d60672c3d60dd527614b20\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:8fb82b9e3fb6ee64820c36c6e219d84bb9edb6eb","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}